WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:18.418
There's something approaching from the distance

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:20.687
that you can't stop.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.323
It can completely destroy cities

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:26.393
and kill tens of thousands of people.

00:00:26.393 --> 00:00:29.062
It looks like another big wave at first,

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:32.098
but as it gets closer, you can see

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:34.034
it's not just big.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:36.169
It's HUGE!

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:38.705
It's a tsunami.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:41.508
You should probably run away from it,

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:42.776
but instead,

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.545
we're going to surf it.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:48.681
Seriously? This can't be good.

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:58.124
Well, we better get started.

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.034
The biggest wave that someone has surfed on

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:11.071
was 24.4 meters tall (80 feet).

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:13.273
Tsunamis can be around this height

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:16.643
but for our purposes let's go a little bigger

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.646
and say ours is 30 meters tall (100 feet).

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.981
But if you do want to surf on a tsunami

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.217
- and we don't recommend you do it ,

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.786
you'll most likely have to travel to Japan

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.054
or the Philippines.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.156
This is because they're located

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.925
in the Ring of Fire,

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:36.129
an area in the Pacific Ocean where
tsunamis are most likely to occur.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.530
But wait,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.934
how do tsunamis occur anyways?

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.603
Tsunamis are different from the usual waves

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:45.371
caused by wind and tides.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:48.241
Tsunamis form due to volcanic activity,

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:52.412
but most often after an
earthquake occurs in the ocean.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.081
If an earthquake happens in the sea that's

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:57.717
6.5 or higher on the Richter scale,

00:01:57.717 --> 00:02:01.287
you can expect a tsunami to come heading for land. 

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.123
This massive wave will start

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.624
deep in the ocean and

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.426
build its way up to the coastline,

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.928
quickly gaining speed and power.

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.197
Experts say that a tsunami can move up to

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.400
800 km/h (497 mph) as it reaches the shore,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.140
so be prepared for a wave as fast a passenger jet.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.043
And although tsunamis start
as small bumps deep in the ocean,

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.879
as they reach shallower water

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:28.047
they become much more massive.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:31.050
So how would you surf on this thing?

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.053
Let's hope you have a friend
that's just as daring as you.

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.823
Because the wave is moving so fast,

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:39.893
you'd need your friend to drive
up the wave on a jet ski

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.028
while you hitch a ride on the back. 

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.663
If you manage to get to the top,

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.131
you'll quickly realize that

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.300
this wave is completely different

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.502
from any other wave you've surfed before.

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:52.772
That's because tsunami waves don't break

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:56.276
as opposed to regular waves created by wind.

00:02:56.276 --> 00:03:00.013
A breaking wave curves and cascades onto itself,

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:03.149
making it the perfect wall for your surfboard.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.484
But generally speaking,

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:07.086
a tsunami doesn't have this quality.

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.821
Apart from the massive size,

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:11.224
and the incredible speed of the tsunami,

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:13.893
surfing on it would be nearly impossible because

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:16.863
there wouldn't be much for your board to grip onto.

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:20.333
But you're up here now, so what do you do?

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.868
Well, your best bet

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:25.305
is to really only hold on for your
dear life and hope for the best.

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:26.339
Let's be real here;

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:29.742
there's no way you're going to
be able to surf on this monster.

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:34.214
You'll quickly be hurled towards whatever
coast the tsunami decides to bring you to.

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:37.584
And you probably won't just land on the beach either.

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.786
You could be several hundred meters or

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:41.854
even kilometers inland.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:44.023
It could hurl you against the side of a bus,

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.925
or into a building.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:48.361
And after the tsunami brings you there

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.663
that won't be all that happens.

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:52.565
After it tosses you inland,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.501
the waves will pull you right back out into the ocean.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:58.104
Now it's not only you in the tsunami.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:59.072
Other people,

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:03.643
cars, and even entire parts of buildings
will all be forced into the ocean,

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:07.780
causing even more destruction
as they slosh through the tsunami's path.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.549
You may think it's over now,

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.384
but that isn't the case.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.020
A tsunami isn't just one massive wave;

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.855
it's multiple waves.

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:18.858
A tsunami can last for up to an hour.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:20.760
With waves up to 30 meters (100 feet)

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.265
continually hitting the coast while
destroying cities and killing people.

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.300
So once you lose your surfboard,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.869
you'd be in the water with everything else,

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.706
being pushed back and forth
from the coast and back out to the sea.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:36.142
You'd keep doing this until you inevitably drown

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:39.212
or die from getting hit by debris.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:43.516
So although surfing a tsunami
would make for a pretty gnarly story,

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:46.719
maybe we should stick to regular breakers for now. 

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:50.356
One person who might be a
contender to tame a tsunami

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.293
would be legendary Big Wave Surfer Laird Hamilton.

00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:59.766
